The code statements that lead to the bug are:

```
if (sinfo->nr_frags == MAX_SKB_FRAGS) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto out;
}
if (!page) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto out;
}
if (copy_from_user(page_address(page), data_in + size, data_len)) {
    ret = -EFAULT;
    goto out;
}
```

Reason:
These three error-handling `goto out` statements are executed after `xdp_convert_md_to_buff()` has obtained a reference to the netdevice (via `ctx`). Jumping directly to `out` skips the call to `xdp_convert_buff_to_md()`, which is responsible for releasing that reference. As a result, the reference count is leaked, eventually causing the device to not become free (as reported by syzbot).

The code statements that fix the bug are:

```
if (sinfo->nr_frags == MAX_SKB_FRAGS) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto out_put_dev;
}
if (!page) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto out_put_dev;
}
if (copy_from_user(page_address(page), data_in + size, data_len)) {
    ret = -EFAULT;
    goto out_put_dev;
}
...
out_put_dev:
    xdp_convert_buff_to_md(&xdp, ctx);
    if (ret)
        goto out;
```

Reason:
The three error paths now jump to a new label `out_put_dev` instead of `out`. At `out_put_dev`, `xdp_convert_buff_to_md()` is called to release the netdevice reference obtained earlier by `xdp_convert_md_to_buff()`. This ensures that the reference count is properly decremented even if the test run fails, fixing the leak. The conversion is moved before checking the return code so that the reference is always released.

